    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Identity Information — Email Addresses", "technique": "T1589.002", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-12 09:14:07 CEST, Suricata logged the first of a sustained burst of 'ET WEB_SERVER Suspicious User Enumeration Pattern' alerts with data.srcip=198.51.100.42 targeting data.dstip=10.108.57.177 on data.dstport=80. The requests hit the URL path /api/users/search?q=<firstname>.<lastname> — a user-search API endpoint — iterating through a dictionary of common corporate firstname.lastname combinations (john.smith, alice.jones, bob.brown ...). The request rate was deliberately throttled to roughly one query every 8 seconds, slow enough to slip under naive WAF rate-limit thresholds, but the sequential firstname.lastname dictionary pattern still tripped the Suricata user-enumeration signature. The attacker is probing which username patterns the application recognises so it can build a validated target list.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signature 'ET WEB_SERVER Suspicious User Enumeration Pattern' triggered repeatedly with a single external data.srcip=198.51.100.42 against data.dstip=10.108.57.177 on data.url matching the wildcard pattern */api/users/search?q=* with sequentially varying firstname.lastname query values.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:14:07+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Identity Information — Email Addresses", "technique": "T1589.002", "what_happened": "At 09:23:30 CEST, after a short jittered pause, the same data.srcip=198.51.100.42 shifted Phase 2: instead of bare usernames it began submitting full email addresses to the same endpoint via the email parameter — /api/users/search?email=<firstname>.<lastname>@corp.local — testing which firstname.lastname@corp.local addresses the directory considers valid. Suricata continued firing the user-enumeration signature. The user-agent stayed constant throughout both phases: 'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/120.0.0.0 Safari/537.36' — a single scripted client masquerading as a desktop browser.", "chain_reason": "Same data.srcip=198.51.100.42, same data.dstip=10.108.57.177, same constant data.http.user_agent, but data.url shifts from the */api/users/search?q=* pattern to the */api/users/search?email=*%40corp.local pattern — the operator pivoted from username-format discovery to email-address validation against the same endpoint within one continuous session.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:23:30+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Cumulative Volume", "technique": "T1589.002", "what_happened": "Across the 09:14-09:30 window (~15 minutes), Wazuh aggregated dozens of distinct 'ET WEB_SERVER Suspicious User Enumeration Pattern' alerts all sharing data.srcip=198.51.100.42, data.dstip=10.108.57.177 and the constant Chrome/120 user-agent — combining the Phase-1 username-format probing and the Phase-2 email-validation probing into one continuous identity-harvesting session against the /api/users/search endpoint. The polite ~8-second-per-query throttling plus the firstname.lastname dictionary structure plus the q-then-email parameter pivot is the canonical Gather Victim Identity Information — Email Addresses (T1589.002) fingerprint: human-focused reconnaissance aimed at building a high-fidelity target list for a follow-on phishing campaign.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh correlation rule 'Sustained web user-enumeration activity from single external source' fired on aggregate count of ET WEB_SERVER user-enumeration alerts from the same data.srcip within a short window against a single application endpoint. The MITRE mapping T1589.002 (Gather Victim Identity Information: Email Addresses) was attached automatically by the rule.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:29:00+0200"}
